  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias across the whole horizon: forecasts systematically underpredict the realized level by roughly +2 to +10 €/t, with the gap often widening during strong uptrends.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the market breaks higher, the forecast path follows the direction but with insufficient slope (underestimates momentum), especially in the first ~5–20 days.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: mid-horizon (≈day 7–25) errors are typically larger than day 1–3, indicating the model fails to carry forward sustained rallies.",
    "Over-imposed mean reversion: forecasts drift back toward ~70–75 even when the true series holds in the high-70s/80s; reduce mean-reversion strength when recent history shows a regime shift.",
    "Late-horizon drawdown mis-timing: in examples where the true series eventually rolls over (end of Jan window), the model often predicts the decline too early and/or too deep; delay and dampen late-horizon pullbacks after a strong rally."
  ]
}
